WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.390 --> 00:00:10.770
My quarantine fad of choice
this last summer was gardening.

00:00:10.770 --> 00:00:13.410
Among other things, I was
working with some basil,

00:00:13.410 --> 00:00:16.540
and a friend of mine sent
me a recipe for pesto.

00:00:16.540 --> 00:00:19.620
It turns out, they're one of
those good at cooking types.

00:00:19.620 --> 00:00:22.890
So instead of talking
tablespoons or cups,

00:00:22.890 --> 00:00:25.590
this recipe was all in grams.

00:00:25.590 --> 00:00:27.480
Seeing that I don't
own a scale, I

00:00:27.480 --> 00:00:31.020
realized that I had stumbled
upon a totally real world

00:00:31.020 --> 00:00:34.590
definitely not contrived
need for machine learning.

00:00:34.590 --> 00:00:37.140
I know how tall the plants
are, what species they

00:00:37.140 --> 00:00:40.920
are, and some other yada
yada about the concentration

00:00:40.920 --> 00:00:42.720
of some sort of chemical.

00:00:42.720 --> 00:00:46.680
And I need to estimate
my basil mass.

00:00:46.680 --> 00:00:48.870
Thankfully, the previous
tenant of 10 years

00:00:48.870 --> 00:00:51.660
has been collecting
measurements on this same plot,

00:00:51.660 --> 00:00:54.160
so I have some solid
data to work with.

00:00:54.160 --> 00:00:56.250
So what's our strategy?

00:00:56.250 --> 00:00:58.170
Maybe there's no need
for anything fancy,

00:00:58.170 --> 00:01:01.140
we could start out with
a linear regression.

00:01:01.140 --> 00:01:04.290
And maybe I'm the kind of fellow
that uses KNN in the regression

00:01:04.290 --> 00:01:07.140
setting, we can run with that.

00:01:07.140 --> 00:01:09.540
And maybe I'm feeling trendy,
and decide that my best

00:01:09.540 --> 00:01:12.510
move is a neural network.

00:01:12.510 --> 00:01:15.480
Or OK, hear me out.

00:01:15.480 --> 00:01:19.620
What if I could figure out some
way to combine those outputs.

00:01:19.620 --> 00:01:21.900
I want to come up with
some sort of model that

00:01:21.900 --> 00:01:24.690
generates predictions
that are formed by each

00:01:24.690 --> 00:01:27.150
of these constituent models.

00:01:27.150 --> 00:01:29.640
This is the premise
behind ensembling.

00:01:29.640 --> 00:01:31.740
Ensembling is a machine
learning technique

00:01:31.740 --> 00:01:34.320
that has been shown to
increase predictive performance

00:01:34.320 --> 00:01:36.180
in a variety of settings.

00:01:36.180 --> 00:01:38.460
In a community survey
conducted over the summer

00:01:38.460 --> 00:01:40.350
by the Tidymodels
team, we learned

00:01:40.350 --> 00:01:41.850
that users felt
that the number one

00:01:41.850 --> 00:01:44.850
priority for future
development in this ecosystem

00:01:44.850 --> 00:01:47.040
should be ensembling.

00:01:47.040 --> 00:01:49.890
This December, we are delighted
to announce the initial release

00:01:49.890 --> 00:01:52.350
of stacks of package
for Tidymodels

00:01:52.350 --> 00:01:54.840
aligned model ensembling.

00:01:54.840 --> 00:01:57.210
When you're coding up
an ensemble of stacks,

00:01:57.210 --> 00:01:58.830
most of the code
you'll end up writing

00:01:58.830 --> 00:02:01.163
isn't specific to stacks at all.

00:02:01.163 --> 00:02:03.330
I'll skip over the code
that generated these models,

00:02:03.330 --> 00:02:05.340
but the gist is that
I've created three model

00:02:05.340 --> 00:02:07.890
specifications here
using functionality

00:02:07.890 --> 00:02:11.160
from the Tidymodel's core
packages like parsnip,

00:02:11.160 --> 00:02:14.910
workflows, recipes, and tune.

00:02:14.910 --> 00:02:17.130
The linear regression
spec here just defines

00:02:17.130 --> 00:02:18.780
one model definition.

00:02:18.780 --> 00:02:21.660
There aren't any hyper
parameters to tune.

00:02:21.660 --> 00:02:24.540
For this KNN model
spec though, I'm

00:02:24.540 --> 00:02:27.060
tuning over several possible
numbers of neighbors.

00:02:27.060 --> 00:02:30.870
So this spec actually defines
four model definitions.

00:02:30.870 --> 00:02:33.000
Finally, for this
neural network model,

00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:34.890
I'm tuning over
six combinations

00:02:34.890 --> 00:02:37.410
of numbers of hidden
layers and dropout rates.

00:02:37.410 --> 00:02:40.920
So this spec defines
six model definitions.

00:02:40.920 --> 00:02:43.560
And stacks each of these
11 model definitions

00:02:43.560 --> 00:02:47.160
are called candidate members
or "candidates" for short.

00:02:47.160 --> 00:02:48.900
These candidates are
the building blocks

00:02:48.900 --> 00:02:50.670
for an ensemble.

00:02:50.670 --> 00:02:52.860
The first step to putting
together a model stack

00:02:52.860 --> 00:02:55.200
is the stacks
initialization function,

00:02:55.200 --> 00:02:59.310
which works kind of like the
ggplot function from ggplot2.

00:02:59.310 --> 00:03:02.310
It lays the foundation called
a data stack for the model

00:03:02.310 --> 00:03:04.950
to be built on top of.

00:03:04.950 --> 00:03:08.280
Now, the first core of urban
stacks is `add_candidates()`.

00:03:08.280 --> 00:03:12.180
It takes in a
candidate and adds it.

00:03:12.180 --> 00:03:14.670
We can iteratively call
the function as many times

00:03:14.670 --> 00:03:17.640
as we need to add all
of the candidates.

00:03:17.640 --> 00:03:19.860
Under the hood,
`add_candidates()` collects

00:03:19.860 --> 00:03:22.020
predictions on a subset
of the training data

00:03:22.020 --> 00:03:23.280
from each candidate.

00:03:23.280 --> 00:03:26.970
And appends them as
columns to the data stack.

00:03:26.970 --> 00:03:29.220
The second core verb
is `blend_predictions()`,

00:03:29.220 --> 00:03:32.250
which fits a regularized
linear model on the data

00:03:32.250 --> 00:03:34.740
stack predicting the
true outcome using

00:03:34.740 --> 00:03:38.040
the predictions from each
of the candidate members.

00:03:38.040 --> 00:03:40.740
We call the coefficients of
this linear model stacking

00:03:40.740 --> 00:03:42.210
coefficients.

00:03:42.210 --> 00:03:44.340
A stacking
coefficient of 0 means

00:03:44.340 --> 00:03:46.770
that the predictions for
the relevant candidate

00:03:46.770 --> 00:03:50.850
will not ultimately influence
the models stacks predictions.

00:03:50.850 --> 00:03:53.730
Candidate members with
non-zero stacking coefficients

00:03:53.730 --> 00:03:55.680
earn the title of
members, and we

00:03:55.680 --> 00:03:57.780
can fit them on the
whole training data using

00:03:57.780 --> 00:04:00.420
the fit member's function.

00:04:00.420 --> 00:04:02.520
And that's it, the
output of fit members

00:04:02.520 --> 00:04:05.010
is ready to predict on new data.

00:04:05.010 --> 00:04:08.390
Fingers crossed that I'll
nail this pesto recipe.

